\chapter{Polyglot Compilation and the Frankenstein Toolchain}
\label{ch:polyglot}

Several design choices in this document — filesystem servers in
``Haskell, Mercury, or Koka'' (\S\ref{sec:fs_language_abstraction}),
personality servers as separate processes that could be written in any
language, the M:N executor's dependence on Rust \texttt{async} — assume
that multiple source languages can coexist in a single system image.
That assumption is not gratuitous: it is backed by a separate
toolchain project, \textbf{Frankenstein} and its companion
\textbf{Organ Bank}, which together constitute a polyglot compiler
pipeline targeting exactly the safe, capability-enforcing, CPS-friendly
execution model Telix describes.

This chapter shines a brief spotlight on them.  Neither project is
load-bearing for the Telix design: a single-language Rust
implementation is the fallback.  But they make \emph{real} the
ambition, expressed in \S\ref{sec:fs_language_abstraction}, of writing
filesystem and network logic in languages whose concurrency models
match the problem rather than wrestling every driver into Rust.

\section{Organ Bank: Harvesting Compiler Frontends}

\textbf{Organ Bank} is a collection of shims, one per source language,
that wrap real, battle-tested compiler frontends and extract their
typed intermediate representations after frontend analysis.  The
harvested IR is emitted in a common JSON interchange format called
\textbf{OrganIR}.  Shims exist (or are scaffolded) for:

\begin{quote}
GHC (Haskell), rustc (Rust), mmc (Mercury), Idris~2, Lean~4, Koka,
OCaml, Erlang/Elixir, PureScript, Swift, Agda, F\#, Scala~3, Julia,
Zig, C, C++, Fortran, Ada, Standard ML, Common Lisp, Scheme, and
Prolog.
\end{quote}

Rather than writing twenty-three parsers and twenty-three type
checkers, Organ Bank reuses the real compilers as \emph{donors}.  Each
shim invokes its host compiler's frontend, intercepts the typed AST or
core IR, normalises it to OrganIR, and hands it off.  The name is
deliberate: it is a harvesting facility for compiler organs, each one
alive in its own right.

\section{Frankenstein: Stitching Organs Together}

\textbf{Frankenstein} is the polyglot compiler that consumes OrganIR
from multiple frontends and stitches the pieces into a single native
binary.  Its internal pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A shared \textbf{Core IR} (inspired by Koka's Core, with
    Perceus reference counting) receives the typed IR from each organ.
  \item A cross-language linking phase resolves symbols and constructs
    a unified call graph.
  \item The Core IR is lowered to \textbf{MLIR} and then to
    \textbf{LLVM} for native code generation.
\end{enumerate}

The philosophy is stated in its README: ``Like the original monster,
Frankenstein is assembled from donor parts — each one alive in its own
right.''

\section{How This Serves Telix}

The connection to Telix is not architectural but \emph{ecological}.
None of Telix's claims depend on polyglot compilation.  But three
design positions are \emph{amplified} by it:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Algebraic-effect filesystems.}] \S\ref{sec:fs_language_abstraction}
    argues that moving the VFS to userspace lets storage servers be
    written in languages where async I/O is an algebraic effect
    (Koka, Haskell with effect libraries) rather than hand-rolled
    state machines.  Organ Bank is what makes ``write the filesystem
    in Koka'' an engineering path rather than a thought experiment.

  \item[\textbf{Personality servers in their native languages.}]
    A Windows personality could be a Rust process; a Linux personality
    could be a Rust process.  But Organ Bank enables a Linux
    personality written in Mercury (logic-programming strength for
    filesystem traversal), a network stack in Haskell (declarative
    protocol state machines), and a device driver in Zig (fine-grained
    layout control).  Each runs as an isolated, untrusted server.

  \item[\textbf{Verification decomposition.}] The verification
    strategy (\S\ref{ch:verification}) verifies the framekernel core
    in RefinedRust; external servers are verified separately or not at
    all.  If a server is written in Idris~2 or Lean~4 — languages with
    dependent types and native proof capabilities — its correctness
    can be proved in its \emph{own} proof assistant, independent of
    the Rocq/Iris stack, and the only inter-language obligation is the
    capability-channel protocol.  Organ Bank's OrganIR provides the
    typed interchange layer that makes this decomposition mechanical.
\end{description}

\section{Limitations and Status}

As of 2026, Frankenstein and Organ Bank are functional for polyglot
demos and self-host bootstrap, but do not yet enforce a cross-language
type discipline at FFI boundaries: every cross-language call is a
uniform \texttt{i64} ABI with symbol-name resolution, and soundness
rests on the demo author's discipline.  A cross-language FFI type
checker — operating at the OrganIR level to verify that argument and
return types agree across the language boundary — is the next design
milestone.

This is not a defect for Telix, because Telix's capability boundaries
are already enforced at the \emph{message} level: a personality server
receives capability tokens, not raw pointers, and the IOMMU/MMU
enforces spatial bounds regardless of what language the server was
written in.  Frankenstein's type discipline across language boundaries
is an additional safety layer, not a prerequisite.

\section{The Singularity Lineage}

The lineage is deliberate.  Singularity \footcite{hunt07singularity}
demonstrated that a whole OS could be written in a safe language
(Sing\#) with sealed processes and typed IPC.  Midori extended the
idea to a production system in C\#.  Telix updates the lineage to
Rust, and Frankenstein takes the next step: \emph{any} safe language,
stitched together by a common compiler pipeline.  The three projects
— Telix, Frankenstein, and Organ Bank — are independent efforts that
reinforce each other but are not coupled: any one of them delivers
value alone; together they enable a polyglot, capability-enforcing,
formally decomposable system software stack.